.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CONF_CMD 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_CONF_cmd_value_type ,
.Nm SSL_CONF_cmd
.Nd send configuration command
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CONF_cmd
.Fa "SSL_CONF_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const char *option"
.Fa "const char *value"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CONF_cmd_value_type
.Fa "SSL_CONF_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const char *option"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The function
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
performs configuration operation
.Fa option
with optional parameter
.Fa value
on
.Fa ctx .
Its purpose is to simplify application configuration of
.Sy SSL_CTX
or
.Sy SSL
structures by providing a common framework for command line options or
configuration files.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd_value_type
returns the type of value that
.Fa option
refers to.
.Sh SUPPORTED COMMAND LINE COMMANDS
Currently supported
.Fa option
names for command lines (i.e.
when the flag
.Sy SSL_CONF_FLAG_CMDLINE
is set) are listed below.
Note: all
.Fa option
names are case sensitive.
Unless otherwise stated commands can be used by both clients and servers
and the
.Fa value
parameter is not used.
The default prefix for command line commands is
.Sy -
and that is reflected below.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -bugs
Various bug workarounds are set, same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_ALL .
.It Sy -no_comp
Disables support for SSL/TLS compression, same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_COMPRESSION .
As of OpenSSL 1.1.0, compression is off by default.
.It Sy -comp
Enables support for SSL/TLS compression, same as clearing
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_COMPRESSION .
This command was introduced in OpenSSL 1.1.0. As of OpenSSL 1.1.0,
compression is off by default.
TLS compression can only be used in security level 1 or lower.
From OpenSSL 3.2.0 and above the default security level is 2, so this
option will have no effect without also changing the security level.
See
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_security_level 3 .
.It Sy -no_ticket
Disables support for session tickets, same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TICKET .
.It Sy -serverpref
Use server and not client preference order when determining which cipher
suite, signature algorithm or elliptic curve to use for an incoming
connection.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_CIPHER_SERVER_PREFERENCE .
Only used by servers.
.It Sy -client_renegotiation
Allows servers to accept client-initiated renegotiation.
Equivalent to setting
.Sy SSL_OP_ALLOW_CLIENT_RENEGOTIATION .
Only used by servers.
.It Sy -legacy_renegotiation
Permits the use of unsafe legacy renegotiation.
Equivalent to setting
.Sy SSL_OP_ALLOW_UNSAFE_LEGACY_RENEGOTIATION .
.It Sy -no_renegotiation
Disables all attempts at renegotiation in (D)TLSv1.2 and earlier, same
as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_RENEGOTIATION .
.It Sy -no_resumption_on_reneg
Sets
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_SESSION_RESUMPTION_ON_RENEGOTIATION .
Only used by servers.
.It Sy -legacy_server_connect Ns , Sy -no_legacy_server_connect
Permits or prohibits the use of unsafe legacy renegotiation for OpenSSL
clients only.
Equivalent to setting or clearing
.Sy SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT .
.It Sy -prioritize_chacha
Prioritize ChaCha ciphers when the client has a ChaCha20 cipher at the
top of its preference list.
This usually indicates a client without AES hardware acceleration (e.g.
mobile) is in use.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_PRIORITIZE_CHACHA .
Only used by servers.
Requires
.Sy -serverpref .
.It Sy -allow_no_dhe_kex
In TLSv1.3 allow a non-(ec)dhe based key exchange mode on resumption.
This means that there will be no forward secrecy for the resumed
session.
.It Sy -prefer_no_dhe_kex
In TLSv1.3, on resumption let the server prefer a non-(ec)dhe based key
exchange mode over an (ec)dhe based one.
Requires
.Sy -allow_no_dhe_kex .
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_PREFER_NO_DHE_KEX .
Only used by servers.
.It Sy -strict
Enables strict mode protocol handling.
Equivalent to setting
.Sy SSL_CERT_FLAG_TLS_STRICT .
.It Sy -sigalgs Em algs
This sets the supported signature algorithms for TLSv1.2 and TLSv1.3.
For clients this value is used directly for the supported signature
algorithms extension.
For servers it is used to determine which signature algorithms to
support.
.Pp
The
.Sy algs
argument should be a colon separated list of signature algorithms in
order of decreasing preference of the form
.Sy algorithm+hash
or
.Sy signature_scheme .
For the default providers shipped with OpenSSL,
.Sy algorithm
is one of
.Sy RSA ,
.Sy DSA
or
.Sy ECDSA
and
.Sy hash
is a supported algorithm OID short name such as
.Sy SHA1 ,
.Sy SHA224 ,
.Sy SHA256 ,
.Sy SHA384
or
.Sy SHA512 .
.Sy signature_scheme
is one of the signature schemes defined in TLSv1.3, specified using the
IETF name, e.g.,
.Sy ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 ,
.Sy ed25519 ,
or
.Sy rsa_pss_pss_sha256 .
Additional providers may make available further algorithms via the
TLS-SIGALG capability.
Signature scheme names and public key algorithm names (but not the hash
names) in the
.Sy algorithm+hash
form are case-insensitive.
See
.Xr provider-base 7 .
.Pp
If this option is not set then all signature algorithms supported by all
activated providers are permissible.
.Pp
Note: algorithms which specify a PKCS#1 v1.5 signature scheme (either by
using
.Sy RSA
as the
.Sy algorithm
or by using one of the
.Sy rsa_pkcs1_*
identifiers) are ignored in TLSv1.3 and will not be negotiated.
.It Sy -client_sigalgs Em algs
This sets the supported signature algorithms associated with client
authentication for TLSv1.2 and TLSv1.3. For servers the
.Sy algs
is used in the
.Sy signature_algorithms
field of a
.Sy CertificateRequest
message.
For clients it is used to determine which signature algorithm to use
with the client certificate.
If a server does not request a certificate this option has no effect.
.Pp
The syntax of
.Sy algs
is identical to
.Sy -sigalgs .
If not set, then the value set for
.Sy -sigalgs
will be used instead.
.It Sy -groups Em groups
This sets the supported groups.
For clients, the groups are sent using the supported groups extension.
For servers, it is used to determine which group to use.
This setting affects groups used for signatures (in TLSv1.2 and earlier)
and key exchange.
.Pp
In its simplest form the
.Em groups
argument is a colon separated list of groups.
The preferred names are those listed in the IANA
.Lk https://www.iana.org/assignments/tls-parameters/tls-parameters.xhtml#tls-parameters-8
registry.
.Pp
For some groups, OpenSSL supports additional aliases.
Such an alias could be a
.Sy NIST
name (e.g.
.Sy P-256 Ns ),
an OpenSSL OID name (e.g.
.Sy prime256v1 Ns ),
or some other commonly used name.
Group names are case-insensitive in OpenSSL 3.5 and later.
The list should be in order of preference with the most preferred group
first.
.Pp
The first group listed will also be used for the
.Sy key_share
sent by a client in a TLSv1.3
.Sy ClientHello .
.Pp
The commands below list the IANA names for TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3,
respectively:
.Bd -literal
    $ openssl list -tls1_2 -tls-groups
    $ openssl list -tls1_3 -tls-groups
.Ed
.Pp
The recommended groups (in order of decreasing performance) for TLS 1.3
are presently:
.Pp
.Sy x25519 ,
.Sy secp256r1 ,
.Sy x448 ,
and
.Sy secp384r1 .
.Pp
The stronger security margins of the last two, come at a significant
performance penalty.
.Pp
An enriched alternative syntax, that enables clients to send multiple
keyshares and allows servers to prioritise some groups over others, is
described in
.Xr SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list 3 .
Since TLS 1.2 has neither keyshares nor a hello retry mechanism, with
TLS 1.2 the enriched syntax is ultimately equivalent to just a simple
ordered list of groups, as with the simple form above.
.It Sy -curves Em groups
This is a synonym for the
.Sy -groups
command.
.It Sy -named_curve Em curve
This sets the temporary curve used for ephemeral ECDH modes.
This is only applicable in TLS 1.0 and 1.1, and should not be used with
later protocol versions.
.Pp
The
.Em curve
argument is a curve name or the special value
.Sy auto
which picks an appropriate curve based on client and server preferences.
The curve can be either the
.Sy NIST
name (e.g.
.Sy P-256 )
or an OpenSSL OID name (e.g.
.Sy prime256v1 Ns ).
Even with TLS 1.0 and 1.1, the default value of
.Qo Li auto Qc
is strongly recommended over choosing a specific curve.
Curve names are case-insensitive in OpenSSL 3.5 and later.
.It Sy -tx_cert_comp
Enables support for sending TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -no_tx_cert_comp
Disables support for sending TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -rx_cert_comp
Enables support for receiving TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -no_rx_cert_comp
Disables support for receiving TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -comp
.It Sy -cipher Em ciphers
Sets the TLSv1.2 and below ciphersuite list to
.Sy ciphers .
This list will be combined with any configured TLSv1.3 ciphersuites.
Note: syntax checking of
.Sy ciphers
is currently not performed unless a
.Sy SSL
or
.Sy SSL_CTX
structure is associated with
.Fa ctx .
.It Sy -ciphersuites Em 1.3ciphers
Sets the available ciphersuites for TLSv1.3 to value.
This is a colon-separated list of TLSv1.3 ciphersuite names in order of
preference.
This list will be combined any configured TLSv1.2 and below
ciphersuites.
See
.Xr openssl-ciphers 1
for more information.
.It Sy -min_protocol Em minprot Ns , Sy -max_protocol Em maxprot
Sets the minimum and maximum supported protocol.
Currently supported protocol values are
.Sy SSLv3 ,
.Sy TLSv1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.2 ,
.Sy TLSv1.3
for TLS;
.Sy DTLSv1 ,
.Sy DTLSv1.2
for DTLS, and
.Sy None
for no limit.
If either the lower or upper bound is not specified then only the other
bound applies, if specified.
If your application supports both TLS and DTLS you can specify any of
these options twice, once with a bound for TLS and again with an
appropriate bound for DTLS. To restrict the supported protocol versions
use these commands rather than the deprecated alternative commands
below.
.It Sy -record_padding Em padding
Controls use of TLSv1.3 record layer padding.
.Sy padding
is a string of the form "number[,number]" where the (required) first
number is the padding block size (in octets) for application data, and
the optional second number is the padding block size for handshake and
alert messages.
If the optional second number is omitted, the same padding will be
applied to all messages.
.Pp
Padding attempts to pad TLSv1.3 records so that they are a multiple of
the set length on send.
A value of 0 or 1 turns off padding as relevant.
Otherwise, the values must be >1 or <=16384.
.It Sy -debug_broken_protocol
Ignored.
.It Sy -no_middlebox
Turn off "middlebox compatibility", as described below.
.El
.Ss Additional Options
The following options are accepted by
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd ,
but are not processed by the OpenSSL commands.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -cert Em file
Attempts to use
.Sy file
as the certificate for the appropriate context.
It currently uses
.Xr SSL_CTX_use_certificate_chain_file 3
if an
.Sy SSL_CTX
structure is set or
.Xr SSL_use_certificate_file 3
with filetype PEM if an
.Sy SSL
structure is set.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
.It Sy -key Em file
Attempts to use
.Sy file
as the private key for the appropriate context.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
Note: if no
.Sy -key
option is set then a private key is not loaded unless the flag
.Sy SSL_CONF_FLAG_REQUIRE_PRIVATE
is set.
.It Sy -dhparam Em file
Attempts to use
.Sy file
as the set of temporary DH parameters for the appropriate context.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
.It Sy -no_ssl3 Ns , Sy -no_tls1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_2 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_3
Disables protocol support for SSLv3, TLSv1.0, TLSv1.1, TLSv1.2 or
TLSv1.3 by setting the corresponding options
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_SSLv3 ,
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1 ,
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_1 ,
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_2
and
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TLSv1_3
respectively.
These options are deprecated, use
.Sy -min_protocol
and
.Sy -max_protocol
instead.
.It Sy -anti_replay Ns , Sy -no_anti_replay
Switches replay protection, on or off respectively.
With replay protection on, OpenSSL will automatically detect if a
session ticket has been used more than once, TLSv1.3 has been
negotiated, and early data is enabled on the server.
A full handshake is forced if a session ticket is used a second or
subsequent time.
Anti-Replay is on by default unless overridden by a configuration file
and is only used by servers.
Anti-replay measures are required for compliance with the TLSv1.3
specification.
Some applications may be able to mitigate the replay risks in other ways
and in such cases the built-in OpenSSL functionality is not required.
Switching off anti-replay is equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_ANTI_REPLAY .
.El
.Sh SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION FILE COMMANDS
Currently supported
.Fa option
names for configuration files (i.e., when the flag
.Sy SSL_CONF_FLAG_FILE
is set) are listed below.
All configuration file
.Fa option
names are case insensitive so
.Sy signaturealgorithms
is recognised as well as
.Sy SignatureAlgorithms .
Unless otherwise stated the
.Fa value
names are also case insensitive.
.Pp
Note: the command prefix (if set) alters the recognised
.Fa option
values.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy CipherString
Sets the ciphersuite list for TLSv1.2 and below to
.Fa value .
This list will be combined with any configured TLSv1.3 ciphersuites.
Note: syntax checking of
.Fa value
is currently not performed unless an
.Sy SSL
or
.Sy SSL_CTX
structure is associated with
.Fa ctx .
.It Sy Ciphersuites
Sets the available ciphersuites for TLSv1.3 to
.Fa value .
This is a colon-separated list of TLSv1.3 ciphersuite names in order of
preference.
This list will be combined any configured TLSv1.2 and below
ciphersuites.
See
.Xr openssl-ciphers 1
for more information.
.It Sy Certificate
Attempts to use the file
.Fa value
as the certificate for the appropriate context.
It currently uses
.Xr SSL_CTX_use_certificate_chain_file 3
if an
.Sy SSL_CTX
structure is set or
.Xr SSL_use_certificate_file 3
with filetype PEM if an
.Sy SSL
structure is set.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
.It Sy PrivateKey
Attempts to use the file
.Fa value
as the private key for the appropriate context.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
Note: if no
.Sy PrivateKey
option is set then a private key is not loaded unless the
.Sy SSL_CONF_FLAG_REQUIRE_PRIVATE
is set.
.It Sy ChainCAFile Ns , Sy ChainCAPath Ns , Sy VerifyCAFile Ns , Sy VerifyCAPath
These options indicate a file or directory used for building certificate
chains or verifying certificate chains.
These options are only supported if certificate operations are
permitted.
.It Sy RequestCAFile
This option indicates a file containing a set of certificates in PEM
form.
The subject names of the certificates are sent to the peer in the
.Sy certificate_authorities
extension for TLS 1.3 (in ClientHello or CertificateRequest) or in a
certificate request for previous versions or TLS.
.It Sy ServerInfoFile
Attempts to use the file
.Fa value
in the "serverinfo" extension using the function
SSL_CTX_use_serverinfo_file.
.It Sy DHParameters
Attempts to use the file
.Fa value
as the set of temporary DH parameters for the appropriate context.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
.It Sy RecordPadding
Controls use of TLSv1.3 record layer padding.
.Fa value
is a string of the form "number[,number]" where the (required) first
number is the padding block size (in octets) for application data, and
the optional second number is the padding block size for handshake and
alert messages.
If the optional second number is omitted, the same padding will be
applied to all messages.
.Pp
Padding attempts to pad TLSv1.3 records so that they are a multiple of
the set length on send.
A value of 0 or 1 turns off padding as relevant.
Otherwise, the values must be >1 or <=16384.
.It Sy SignatureAlgorithms
This sets the supported signature algorithms for TLSv1.2 and TLSv1.3.
For clients this value is used directly for the supported signature
algorithms extension.
For servers it is used to determine which signature algorithms to
support.
.Pp
The
.Fa value
argument should be a colon separated list of signature algorithms in
order of decreasing preference of the form
.Sy algorithm+hash
or
.Sy signature_scheme .
For the default providers shipped with OpenSSL,
.Sy algorithm
is one of
.Sy RSA ,
.Sy DSA
or
.Sy ECDSA
and
.Sy hash
is a supported algorithm OID short name such as
.Sy SHA1 ,
.Sy SHA224 ,
.Sy SHA256 ,
.Sy SHA384
or
.Sy SHA512 .
.Sy signature_scheme
is one of the signature schemes defined in TLSv1.3, specified using the
IANA name, e.g.,
.Sy ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 ,
.Sy ed25519 ,
or
.Sy rsa_pss_pss_sha256 .
Signature scheme names and public key algorithm names (but not the hash
names) in the
.Sy algorithm+hash
form are case-insensitive.
Additional providers may make available further signature schemes via
the TLS_SIGALG capability.
See
.Xr provider-base 7 .
.Pp
If this option is not set then all signature algorithms supported by all
activated providers are permissible.
.Pp
Note: algorithms which specify a PKCS#1 v1.5 signature scheme (either by
using
.Sy RSA
as the
.Sy algorithm
or by using one of the
.Sy rsa_pkcs1_*
identifiers) are ignored in TLSv1.3 and will not be negotiated.
.It Sy ClientSignatureAlgorithms
This sets the supported signature algorithms associated with client
authentication for TLSv1.2 and TLSv1.3. For servers the value is used in
the
.Sy signature_algorithms
field of a
.Sy CertificateRequest
message.
For clients it is used to determine which signature algorithm to use
with the client certificate.
If a server does not request a certificate this option has no effect.
.Pp
The syntax of
.Fa value
is identical to
.Sy SignatureAlgorithms .
If not set then the value set for
.Sy SignatureAlgorithms
will be used instead.
.It Sy Groups
This sets the supported groups.
For clients, the groups are sent using the supported groups extension.
For servers, it is used to determine which group to use.
This setting affects groups used for signatures (in TLSv1.2 and earlier)
and key exchange.
The first group listed will also be used for the
.Sy key_share
sent by a client in a TLSv1.3
.Sy ClientHello .
.Pp
The
.Sy groups
argument is a colon separated list of groups.
The preferred names are those listed in the IANA
.Lk https://www.iana.org/assignments/tls-parameters/tls-parameters.xhtml#tls-parameters-8
registry.
For some groups, OpenSSL supports additional aliases.
Such an alias could be a
.Sy NIST
name (e.g.
.Sy P-256 Ns ),
an OpenSSL OID name (e.g.
.Sy prime256v1 Ns ),
or some other commonly used name.
Group names are case-insensitive in OpenSSL 3.5 and later.
The list should be in order of preference with the most preferred group
first.
.Pp
The commands below list the available groups for TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3,
respectively:
.Bd -literal
    $ openssl list -tls1_2 -tls-groups
    $ openssl list -tls1_3 -tls-groups
.Ed
.Pp
An enriched alternative syntax, that enables clients to send multiple
keyshares and allows servers to prioritise some groups over others, is
described in
.Xr SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list 3 .
Since TLS 1.2 has neither keyshares nor a hello retry mechanism, with
TLS 1.2 the enriched syntax is ultimately equivalent to just a simple
ordered list of groups, as with the simple form above.
.It Sy Curves
This is a synonym for the "Groups" command.
.It Sy MinProtocol
This sets the minimum supported SSL, TLS or DTLS version.
.Pp
Currently supported protocol values are
.Sy SSLv3 ,
.Sy TLSv1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.2 ,
.Sy TLSv1.3 ,
.Sy DTLSv1
and
.Sy DTLSv1.2 .
The SSL and TLS bounds apply only to TLS-based contexts, while the DTLS
bounds apply only to DTLS-based contexts.
The command can be repeated with one instance setting a TLS bound, and
the other setting a DTLS bound.
The value
.Sy None
applies to both types of contexts and disables the limits.
.It Sy MaxProtocol
This sets the maximum supported SSL, TLS or DTLS version.
.Pp
Currently supported protocol values are
.Sy SSLv3 ,
.Sy TLSv1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.2 ,
.Sy TLSv1.3 ,
.Sy DTLSv1
and
.Sy DTLSv1.2 .
The SSL and TLS bounds apply only to TLS-based contexts, while the DTLS
bounds apply only to DTLS-based contexts.
The command can be repeated with one instance setting a TLS bound, and
the other setting a DTLS bound.
The value
.Sy None
applies to both types of contexts and disables the limits.
.It Sy Protocol
This can be used to enable or disable certain versions of the SSL, TLS
or DTLS protocol.
.Pp
The
.Fa value
argument is a comma separated list of supported protocols to enable or
disable.
If a protocol is preceded by
.Sy -
that version is disabled.
.Pp
All protocol versions are enabled by default.
You need to disable at least one protocol version for this setting have
any effect.
Only enabling some protocol versions does not disable the other protocol
versions.
.Pp
Currently supported protocol values are
.Sy SSLv3 ,
.Sy TLSv1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.1 ,
.Sy TLSv1.2 ,
.Sy TLSv1.3 ,
.Sy DTLSv1
and
.Sy DTLSv1.2 .
The special value
.Sy ALL
refers to all supported versions.
.Pp
This can't enable protocols that are disabled using
.Sy MinProtocol
or
.Sy MaxProtocol ,
but can disable protocols that are still allowed by them.
.Pp
The
.Sy Protocol
command is fragile and deprecated; do not use it.
Use
.Sy MinProtocol
and
.Sy MaxProtocol
instead.
If you do use
.Sy Protocol ,
make sure that the resulting range of enabled protocols has no "holes",
e.g.
if TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.2 are both enabled, make sure to also leave TLS 1.1
enabled.
.It Sy Options
The
.Fa value
argument is a comma separated list of various flags to set.
If a flag string is preceded
.Sy -
it is disabled.
See the
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_options 3
function for more details of individual options.
.Pp
Each option is listed below.
Where an operation is enabled by default the
.Sy -flag
syntax is needed to disable it.
.Pp
.Sy SessionTicket :
session ticket support, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TICKET :
that is
.Sy -SessionTicket
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TICKET .
.Pp
.Sy Compression :
SSL/TLS compression support, disabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_COMPRESSION .
.Pp
.Sy EmptyFragments :
use empty fragments as a countermeasure against a SSL 3.0/TLS 1.0
protocol vulnerability affecting CBC ciphers.
It is set by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_DONT_INSERT_EMPTY_FRAGMENTS .
.Pp
.Sy Bugs :
enable various bug workarounds.
Same as
.Sy SSL_OP_ALL .
.Pp
.Sy DHSingle :
enable single use DH keys, set by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_DH_SINGLE .
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy ECDHSingle :
enable single use ECDH keys, set by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_ECDH_SINGLE .
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy ServerPreference :
use server and not client preference order when determining which cipher
suite, signature algorithm or elliptic curve to use for an incoming
connection.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_CIPHER_SERVER_PREFERENCE .
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy PrioritizeChaCha :
prioritizes ChaCha ciphers when the client has a ChaCha20 cipher at the
top of its preference list.
This usually indicates a mobile client is in use.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_PRIORITIZE_CHACHA .
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy NoResumptionOnRenegotiation :
set
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_SESSION_RESUMPTION_ON_RENEGOTIATION
flag.
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy NoRenegotiation :
disables all attempts at renegotiation in TLSv1.2 and earlier, same as
setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_RENEGOTIATION .
.Pp
.Sy UnsafeLegacyRenegotiation :
permits the use of unsafe legacy renegotiation.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_ALLOW_UNSAFE_LEGACY_RENEGOTIATION .
.Pp
.Sy UnsafeLegacyServerConnect :
permits the use of unsafe legacy renegotiation for OpenSSL clients only.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT .
.Pp
.Sy EncryptThenMac :
use encrypt-then-mac extension, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_ENCRYPT_THEN_MAC :
that is,
.Sy -EncryptThenMac
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_ENCRYPT_THEN_MAC .
.Pp
.Sy AllowNoDHEKEX :
In TLSv1.3 allow a non-(ec)dhe based key exchange mode on resumption.
This means that there will be no forward secrecy for the resumed
session.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_ALLOW_NO_DHE_KEX .
.Pp
.Sy PreferNoDHEKEX :
In TLSv1.3, on resumption let the server prefer a non-(ec)dhe based key
exchange mode over an (ec)dhe based one.
Requires
.Sy AllowNoDHEKEX .
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_PREFER_NO_DHE_KEX .
Only used by servers.
.Pp
.Sy MiddleboxCompat :
If set then dummy Change Cipher Spec (CCS) messages are sent in TLSv1.3.
This has the effect of making TLSv1.3 look more like TLSv1.2 so that
middleboxes that do not understand TLSv1.3 will not drop the connection.
This option is set by default.
A future version of OpenSSL may not set this by default.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_ENABLE_MIDDLEBOX_COMPAT .
.Pp
.Sy AntiReplay :
If set then OpenSSL will automatically detect if a session ticket has
been used more than once, TLSv1.3 has been negotiated, and early data is
enabled on the server.
A full handshake is forced if a session ticket is used a second or
subsequent time.
This option is set by default and is only used by servers.
Anti-replay measures are required to comply with the TLSv1.3
specification.
Some applications may be able to mitigate the replay risks in other ways
and in such cases the built-in OpenSSL functionality is not required.
Disabling anti-replay is equivalent to setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_ANTI_REPLAY .
.Pp
.Sy ExtendedMasterSecret :
use extended master secret extension, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET :
that is,
.Sy -ExtendedMasterSecret
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET .
.Pp
.Sy CANames :
use CA names extension, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_DISABLE_TLSEXT_CA_NAMES :
that is,
.Sy -CANames
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_DISABLE_TLSEXT_CA_NAMES .
.Pp
.Sy KTLS :
Enables kernel TLS if support has been compiled in, and it is supported
by the negotiated ciphersuites and extensions.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_ENABLE_KTLS .
.Pp
.Sy StrictCertCheck :
Enable strict certificate checking.
Equivalent to setting
.Sy SSL_CERT_FLAG_TLS_STRICT
with
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_cert_flags 3 .
.Pp
.Sy TxCertificateCompression :
support sending compressed certificates, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TX_CERTIFICATE_COMPRESSION :
that is,
.Sy -TxCertificateCompression
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_TX_CERTIFICATE_COMPRESSION .
.Pp
.Sy RxCertificateCompression :
support receiving compressed certificates, enabled by default.
Inverse of
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_RX_CERTIFICATE_COMPRESSION :
that is,
.Sy -RxCertificateCompression
is the same as setting
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_RX_CERTIFICATE_COMPRESSION .
.Pp
.Sy KTLSTxZerocopySendfile :
use the zerocopy TX mode of
.Xr sendfile 3 ,
which gives a performance boost when used with KTLS hardware offload.
Note that invalid TLS records might be transmitted if the file is
changed while being sent.
This option has no effect if
.Sy KTLS
is not enabled.
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_ENABLE_KTLS_TX_ZEROCOPY_SENDFILE .
This option only applies to Linux.
KTLS sendfile on FreeBSD doesn't offer an option to disable zerocopy and
always runs in this mode.
.Pp
.Sy IgnoreUnexpectedEOF :
Equivalent to
.Sy SSL_OP_IGNORE_UNEXPECTED_EOF .
You should only enable this option if the protocol running over TLS can
detect a truncation attack itself, and that the application is checking
for that truncation attack.
.It Sy VerifyMode
The
.Fa value
argument is a comma separated list of flags to set.
.Pp
.Sy Peer
enables peer verification: for clients only.
.Pp
.Sy Request
requests but does not require a certificate from the client.
Servers only.
.Pp
.Sy Require
requests and requires a certificate from the client: an error occurs if
the client does not present a certificate.
Servers only.
.Pp
.Sy Once
requests a certificate from a client only on the initial connection: not
when renegotiating.
Servers only.
.Pp
.Sy RequestPostHandshake
configures the connection to support requests but does not require a
certificate from the client post-handshake.
A certificate will not be requested during the initial handshake.
The server application must provide a mechanism to request a certificate
post-handshake.
Servers only.
TLSv1.3 only.
.Pp
.Sy RequiresPostHandshake
configures the connection to support requests and requires a certificate
from the client post-handshake: an error occurs if the client does not
present a certificate.
A certificate will not be requested during the initial handshake.
The server application must provide a mechanism to request a certificate
post-handshake.
Servers only.
TLSv1.3 only.
.It Sy ClientCAFile Ns , Sy ClientCAPath
A file or directory of certificates in PEM format whose names are used
as the set of acceptable names for client CAs.
Servers only.
This option is only supported if certificate operations are permitted.
.El
.Sh SUPPORTED COMMAND TYPES
The function
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd_value_type
currently returns one of the following types:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_UNKNOWN
The
.Fa option
string is unrecognised, this return value can be use to flag syntax
errors.
.It Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_STRING
The value is a string without any specific structure.
.It Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_FILE
The value is a filename.
.It Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_DIR
The value is a directory name.
.It Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_NONE
The value string is not used e.g.
a command line option which doesn't take an argument.
.El
.Sh NOTES
The order of operations is significant.
This can be used to set either defaults or values which cannot be
overridden.
For example if an application calls:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Protocol", "-SSLv3");
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, userparam, uservalue);
.Ed
.Pp
it will disable SSLv3 support by default but the user can override it.
If however the call sequence is:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, userparam, uservalue);
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Protocol", "-SSLv3");
.Ed
.Pp
SSLv3 is
.Sy always
disabled and attempt to override this by the user are ignored.
.Pp
By checking the return code of
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
it is possible to query if a given
.Fa option
is recognised, this is useful if
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
values are mixed with additional application specific operations.
.Pp
For example an application might call
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
and if it returns -2 (unrecognised command) continue with processing of
application specific commands.
.Pp
Applications can also use
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
to process command lines though the utility function
.Xr SSL_CONF_cmd_argv 3
is normally used instead.
One way to do this is to set the prefix to an appropriate value using
.Xr SSL_CONF_CTX_set1_prefix 3 ,
pass the current argument to
.Fa option
and the following argument to
.Fa value
(which may be NULL).
.Pp
In this case if the return value is positive then it is used to skip
that number of arguments as they have been processed by
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd .
If -2 is returned then
.Fa option
is not recognised and application specific arguments can be checked
instead.
If -3 is returned a required argument is missing and an error is
indicated.
If 0 is returned some other error occurred and this can be reported back
to the user.
.Pp
The function
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd_value_type
can be used by applications to check for the existence of a command or
to perform additional syntax checking or translation of the command
value.
For example if the return value is
.Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_FILE
an application could translate a relative pathname to an absolute
pathname.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
returns 1 if the value of
.Fa option
is recognised and
.Fa value
is
.Sy NOT
used and 2 if both
.Fa option
and
.Fa value
are used.
In other words it returns the number of arguments processed.
This is useful when processing command lines.
.Pp
A return value of -2 means
.Fa option
is not recognised.
.Pp
A return value of -3 means
.Fa option
is recognised and the command requires a value but
.Fa value
is NULL.
.Pp
A return code of 0 indicates that both
.Fa option
and
.Fa value
are valid but an error occurred attempting to perform the operation: for
example due to an error in the syntax of
.Fa value
in this case the error queue may provide additional information.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set supported signature algorithms:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "SignatureAlgorithms", "ECDSA+SHA256:RSA+SHA256:DSA+SHA256");
.Ed
.Pp
There are various ways to select the supported protocols.
.Pp
This set the minimum protocol version to TLSv1, and so disables SSLv3.
This is the recommended way to disable protocols.
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "MinProtocol", "TLSv1");
.Ed
.Pp
The following also disables SSLv3:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Protocol", "-SSLv3");
.Ed
.Pp
The following will first enable all protocols, and then disable SSLv3.
If no protocol versions were disabled before this has the same effect as
"-SSLv3", but if some versions were disables this will re-enable them
before disabling SSLv3.
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Protocol", "ALL,-SSLv3");
.Ed
.Pp
Only enable TLSv1.2:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "MinProtocol", "TLSv1.2");
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "MaxProtocol", "TLSv1.2");
.Ed
.Pp
This also only enables TLSv1.2:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Protocol", "-ALL,TLSv1.2");
.Ed
.Pp
Disable TLS session tickets:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Options", "-SessionTicket");
.Ed
.Pp
Enable compression:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Options", "Compression");
.Ed
.Pp
Set supported curves to P-256, P-384:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_CONF_cmd(ctx, "Curves", "P-256:P-384");
.Ed
