.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_CONF_cmd
function was added in OpenSSL 1.0.2.
.Pp
The
.Sy SSL_OP_NO_SSL2
option doesn't have effect since 1.1.0, but the macro is retained for
backwards compatibility.
.Pp
The
.Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_NONE
was added in OpenSSL 1.1.0. In earlier versions of OpenSSL passing a
command which didn't take an argument would return
.Sy SSL_CONF_TYPE_UNKNOWN .
.Pp
.Sy MinProtocol
and
.Sy MaxProtocol
where added in OpenSSL 1.1.0.
.Pp
.Sy AllowNoDHEKEX
and
.Sy PrioritizeChaCha
were added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
.Pp
The
.Sy UnsafeLegacyServerConnect
option is no longer set by default from OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
The
.Sy TxCertificateCompression
and
.Sy RxCertificateCompression
options were added in OpenSSL 3.2.
.Pp
.Sy PreferNoDHEKEX
was added in OpenSSL 3.3.
.Pp
OpenSSL 3.5 introduces support for post-quantum (PQ) TLS key exchange
via the
.Sy MLKEM512 ,
.Sy MLKEM768
and
.Sy MLKEM1024
TLS groups.
These are based on the underlying
.Sy ML-KEM-512 ,
.Sy ML-KEM-768
and
.Sy ML-KEM-1024
algorithms from FIPS 203.
.Pp
OpenSSL 3.5 also introduces support for three
.Sy hybrid
ECDH PQ key exchange TLS groups:
.Sy X25519MLKEM768 ,
.Sy SecP256r1MLKEM768
and
.Sy SecP384r1MLKEM1024 .
They offer CPU performance comparable to the associated ECDH group,
though at the cost of significantly larger key exchange messages.
The third group,
.Sy SecP384r1MLKEM1024
is substantially more CPU-intensive, largely as a result of the high CPU
cost of ECDH for the underlying
.Sy P-384
group.
Also its key exchange messages at close to 1700 bytes are larger than
the roughly 1200 bytes for the first two groups.
.Pp
As of OpenSSL 3.5 key exchange group names are case-insensitive.
